# Multilingual chat invariants

## Emergency bypass
- Emergency keyword check MUST run before RAG retrieval — never after
- Pre-written emergency responses in Kannada/Hindi must NEVER be generated by LLM
- They must be hardcoded strings to guarantee correctness and zero latency
- Emergency responses must always include "108" (Indian emergency number)

## Language handling
- OpenAI client must be module-level singleton — never instantiate per request in `translate_to_english`
- Translation cache uses first 200 chars as key — never cache full long strings
- If detected locale confidence is below `LANGUAGE_DETECTION_MIN_CONFIDENCE` → fall back to English, never guess
- Explicit `ReplyLocale` from the app header/profile is honored for all audiences (including expert / AI diagnosis).
- When no explicit locale is sent, doctor-facing expert flows default to English; layman uses detection + `Accept-Language`.
- Locale codes align with `@saas-builder/i18n-contract`: `en`, `hi`, `kn` (not `english`/`hindi`/`kannada`)

## LLM prompting
- Never concatenate user input directly into system prompt — always in user message role
- The "respond in Kannada/Hindi" instruction goes in the system/prompt template, not user message
- For non-English replies request JSON with `answer` + `answer_english` fields in one call

## Font rendering
- Kannada text must use Noto Sans Kannada — never rely on system font fallback alone
- Hindi text must use Noto Sans Devanagari
- These fonts must be loaded at app startup, not lazily per-message

## Wire contract
- PascalCase API fields: `ReplyLocale`, `DetectedLocale`, `AnswerEnglish`, `ShowTranslationToggle`, `EmergencyCall108`
- RAG corpus stays English; translate query for retrieval via `RetrievalQuestion` only
